      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2010 Elsevier Ireland Ltd. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "Co-mobidity of asthma is known to result in a poor prognosis of post-endoscopic sinus surgery (post-ESS). Bacterial infection may play a key role in recurrent pathophysiology of sinusitis in post-ESS."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Forty-two patients with CRS associated with asthma undergoing ESS were enrolled. Bacterial culture was performed from the sinus cavity at the time of acute infectious episodes. Recurrence of sinonasal disease was analyzed in terms of steroid responsiveness and peak expiratory flow (PEF)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Totally 75 aspirates were obtained during post-ESS; 2 repeat aspirates from 10 patients, 3 from 5 patients, and 4 from 2 patients. Only 6 specimens (8.0%) obtained from 5 patients (11.9%) showed no growth whereas 83 isolates were recovered from 69 specimens. Sixteen patients had at least one episode of a significant decline of PEF. All except one patient complained of symptoms and signs of upper respiratory infections prior to a depression of PEF. Positive culture was obtained in 10 out of 11 patients examined at the time of acute exacerbation of CRS."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Bacterial infection may play a critical role of recurrent polyps and refractory symptoms during post-ESS follow-up. Moreover, worsening of sinusitis accompanies asthma exacerbation."
          }
        ]
      },
